    "alert_id": "DTG_HYPERGLYCEMIA_001",
    "title": "DTG-associated hyperglycemia: pre-diabetes developed",
    "message": "Guidelines require screening for hyperglycemia risk before DTG use and state that known diabetics or those with high blood glucose should not initiate or transition to DTG. DTG has been reported to cause hyperglycemia in previously non-diabetic adults. Since starting DTG 18 months ago, this patient developed impaired fasting glucose (118 mg/dL, up from baseline 92) and pre-diabetic HbA1c (6.1%). She also gained 17 kg and has acanthosis nigricans. Evidence for drug attribution: DTG is a known cause of hyperglycemia, and the onset temporally follows DTG initiation. Evidence against: Family history of diabetes and obesity are independent risk factors. Guidelines recommend glucose monitoring every 3 months for the first year after DTG initiation, which appears overdue.",
    "issue_type": "toxicity",
    "severity": "high",
    "recommended_action": "Assess for diabetes; consider switching to a metabolically neutral ARV (ABC, TAF-based). Reinstate glucose monitoring every 3 months. Refer for diabetes prevention counseling including weight management.",
